Z||000000||VISCERAL||09-12-2001||10:13 PM||||Contemplating using aluminum pillow blocks with poly bushings that completely encircle the sway instead of the clamps that realy clamp halfway around. Would they be an improvement over the flexy clamps with poly?||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||09-12-2001||10:19 PM||||geez, Im not really sure how much the stock u brackets flex to begin with.  After all, its the poly that's going to compress way way before the hardened steel begins to flex.  I'm no suspension guru, but I do know by experience that over stock, just new poly endlinks and pivot bushings/brackets will improve handling, and that you can adjust the rate at which your swaybar transmits energy by tightening the endlinks or loosening them.  This helps when you are running a rear swaybar, like me, so if i want a lot of oversteer i can loosen the front and tighten the rear.  BTW I am using a stock fiero front bar adapted for the rear, very cheap and works great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||VISCERAL||09-12-2001||10:44 PM||||yeah maybe they're really only good for track purposes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||09-13-2001||02:52 AM||||probally no real improvement, there shouldn't be a terrible lot of force there.  Then again, I'm going to be doing something similar to the silver car, just for the HECK of it!!<P>I can see why John called his northstar car 'Obsession".  you get to the point where you want to do EVERYTHING!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
